<description>
Reorder the red cell-center columns by how many filled centers they contain, from fullest to emptiest.

Input structure:
- The grid is a square macro-grid of red 3x3 cells separated by one black vertical gutter column.
- Every macro-cell can differ only at its center pixel, which is either blank or filled.

Rule:
- Treat the center pixels as columns of a binary matrix.
- Count filled centers in each macro-column.
- Move whole center columns into descending count order, preserving each column's vertical pattern and leaving the frame/gutters unchanged.

Key insight:
- The visible swaps are a column sort of the hidden center matrix; no center pixel is created or deleted.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "macro-grid center columns s, each formed by the variable center pixels of the red cells",
  "dst": "left-to-right center-column slots d ordered from most filled centers to fewest",
  "match_cond": "rank(s by filled-center count descending) == rank(d left-to-right)",
  "transform": "copy each matched center column into its slot, preserving its vertical pattern; keep the red frames and black gutters unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid:
    geometry: (2N-1) rows x (4N-1) cols, where N is the macro-grid size
    N: 3-5
    colors: exactly 2 — filled (red/2 [observed]) and blank (black/0 [observed])
  macro_grid:
    layout: NxN square grid of 3x3 cells separated by 1-pixel blank vertical gutters
    frame: all non-center, non-gutter pixels are filled color
    gutters: columns at x=3,7,11,... are entirely blank color
    centers: center pixel (row 1+2r, col 1+4c) of each cell is either filled or blank
  center_matrix:
    size: NxN binary matrix (filled=1, blank=0)
    column_counts: each column has a unique count; counts form a permutation of {0,1,...,N-1}
    total_filled: N*(N-1)/2
    input_order: columns NOT already sorted by descending count
  output:
    transformation: sort center-matrix columns by descending filled-count (left = most filled)
    frame_and_gutters: unchanged from input

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick N uniformly from 3-5
    2. Build the NxN center matrix in sorted (output) form — column c has exactly (N-1-c) filled cells, placed at random row positions
    3. Generate a random permutation of columns that is NOT the identity (descending-sorted) order — this gives the input column arrangement
    4. Construct the full grid — fill all frame pixels with filled color, gutter columns with blank, place center pixels per the shuffled center matrix
    5. Output grid is the same frame with sorted center columns
```
</generation_spec>
